# TF_GLM_FILL_OLDEST_ROWS (issue #199): the oldest waiting prompt takes a share of every multi-prompt chunk. With
# TF_GLM_MULTI_PREFILL=1, _group packed waiting prompts into each prompt chunk shortest first, so a long prompt got
# rows only after every shorter one had its share: under 8 agents (3 Sparks, 2026-11-21) a 100k-200k tool output
# waited a median 93-109 s before its own prefill even ran, with ~4.5 prompts filling at once and the pool 15-67%
# free (not room: a request was held in 6-19% of those waits). next_fill already picks the oldest waiting prompt as
# ``first``; _group only checked it could share a chunk. Now, with TF_GLM_FILL_OLDEST_ROWS=<rows> (a multiple of the
# fill unit up to TF_GLM_FILL_ROWS), ``first`` goes in first and takes up to that many rows, the others follow
# shortest first, and ``first`` takes what they leave. 0 (the default): the packing is unchanged (checked against a
# copy of it on 3,000 random sets). Rank 0 decides; the MFILL message carries the pieces, so the other ranks change
# nothing. Same replies: a token's result does not depend on the chunk it fills in. CPU check:
# tools/fill_oldest_check.py. Applies after 0049 (and 0062's sliced fills, which take the same group).
